  (:adr/body
  "Measured 2026-08-14 against `/Applications/Grok Bot.app` v0.18.0 as installed on this
machine. Method: parse `Contents/Resources/app.asar` (681 entries, 126 MB logical), extract
the 146 non-dependency files, read the bundles. The preload and host bundles are NOT
minified and retain `// src/host/...` module-path comments and JSDoc, so the design
rationale is readable as written.

Nothing here was obtained by running the app against its servers, by intercepting traffic,
or by using any credential. Everything below is from files on disk plus `codesign`, `ps`
and the app's own data directory.

---

## What it is

`CFBundleIdentifier` is `com.anysphere.sand`. Signed `Developer ID Application: Anysphere
Incorporated (DCNK4UB866)`, hardened runtime. `package.json` says `name: sand`,
`author: SpaceXAI`, `homepage: https://cursor.com`. It talks to `api2.cursor.sh`,
`api3.cursor.sh/tev1/v1`, `review.cursor.com`, `metrics.cursor.sh`. `~/Library/Application
Support/Grok Bot/sand-secrets.json` holds `cursor-access-token` / `cursor-refresh-token` /
`cursor-machine-id`.

**`@anysphere/grok-bot` is declared in `package.json` and contributes zero modules to the
shipped bundles.** Counting workspace packages by module-path comment across the four
bundles: proto 425, agent 355, local-exec 277, agent-exec 171, mcp-core 144,
cursor-plugins 109, shell-exec 90, hooks 89, redacted-protos 82 — and grok-bot 0. The
Grok-specific surface is four things, none of them code in this artifact: product naming;
the server-side `aiserver.v1.GrokBotService`; five feature flags (`grok_bot_dynamic_tools`,
`grok_bot_conversation_size_limits`, `grok_bot_conversation_gc`, `grok_bot_connect`,
`grok_primary`); and `SIGN_UP_TYPE_GROK`.

`dist/native/sand-op-launcher` — a signed helper that starts the 1Password CLI after
verifying its code signature (`identifier \"com.1password.op\" and anchor apple generic and
certificate leaf[subject.OU] = \"2BUA8C4S2C\"`) and passes `--vault --account --format=json
--raw --expires-in` — ships in the bundle with **no caller in any bundled JS**. The only
reference to `dist/native` is the WebAuthn signer's path resolution.

**Take: a dependency list is not an inventory of what shipped.** Both findings came from
counting module-path comments in the built artifact, not from reading `package.json`.

## Process topology

    electron-main/main.cjs      17.7 MB   window, update, settings, Cursor auth
      +- electron-preload/*.cjs           IPC bridge (\"dune\" RPC)
    host/host-main.cjs          23.9 MB   agent runtime; 35 extensions
      +- agent-isolation/                 agent-store-worker, transcript-mirror-worker
    node-agent-coordinator      gateway, OAuth, WebAuthn, local-exec relay
    local-exec-daemon/main.cjs   8.6 MB   separate process, separate lifecycle
    renderer/                     22 MB   React 19 + Vite + StyleX + TipTap + noVNC
    native/sand-webauthn-signer  4.3 MB   Rust, CTAP2/FIDO2 over USB HID
    native/sand-op-launcher                1Password CLI launcher (no caller)

`aiserver.v1.GrokBotService` has 30 unary methods plus two server-streaming
(`WatchSandBoxMigration`). Ten carry an `Admin` prefix. The centre of it is
`EnsureSandBox`, whose response hands over the whole box in one shot:

    EnsureSandBoxResponse { cluster, tenant_id, pod_id,
                            network_token,
                            exec_daemon_url, exec_daemon_auth_token,
                            gateway_url,     gateway_token,
                            vnc_url, fork_vnc_base_url,
                            terminals_folder, image_update_available }

Three independent tokens for three planes. `Box` is an interface with several
implementations (`loopback-sand-box`, `shared-desktop-sand-box`); optional capabilities are
absent methods that raise named errors (`BoxEnvironmentSyncUnsupportedError`,
`BoxMcpUnsupportedError`) rather than booleans on a config.

---

## How it stores data

**Three planes, and they are not the same store.**

1. **Per-agent directory, local.** `~/.sand/agents/<agentId>/`. The id is validated as a
   safe folder segment (no `/`, `\\\\`, NUL, `.`, `..`) AND re-checked by resolving and
   asserting the `relative()` result has no separator — belt and braces against traversal.
   Inside: `audit.jsonl` (append-only, local, always written).

2. **SQLite, WAL, checkpointed before it is captured.** `store.db` and
   `conversation-blobs.db` via node's built-in `node:sqlite`. The host tracks a write
   generation and a live-handle count per db path, and runs `PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE)`
   before snapshotting, verifying the fold with `checkpointed >= log` and falling back to
   `stat(db-wal).size === 0`. Sidecars `-wal` / `-shm` / `-journal` are named as a set.

3. **Content-addressed blobs for conversation state.** `agent-kv` stores conversation state
   as blobs addressed by hex id, with typed serdes (`Utf8Serde`; `ProtoSerde` which records
   `typeName` in the blob type). A conversation is a graph of blob references; a missing
   blob is unrecoverable and says so in one sentence with up to three 12-hex-char ids
   (`BlobNotFoundError`). Layers: `cached-blob-store`, `retry-blob-store`,
   `writethrough-middleware`, `typed-blob-store`, `controlled`.

**Sync to the backend uses S3 ETag as the conditional write**, normalized with
`stripS3EtagQuotes` + lowercase, guarded by an OS-level `store-lock`, with unresolved
conflicts appended to `.sync/conflict-events.jsonl` (and `.pending.jsonl`), directory mode
0700. Store source ids are classified `user` / `team` / `cloud` / bare-uuid — the same
store can be owned at three scopes.

**Audit is dual-written and filtered.** Local `audit.jsonl` always; the backend outbox
(`~/.sand/agents/audit-outbox.json`) batches 50, flushes every 5 s, caps at 2000 pending,
backs off 30 s honouring `Retry-After`. `isBackendForwardable` sends an MCP tool call only
when its transport is `stdio`. Audited action kinds: `mcpToolCall`, `shellCommand`,
`browserNavigation`, `computerUseSession`, each a typed `SandAuditEvent` with
`{eventId, occurredAtMs, agentId, turnId, boxId}`.

## How it shares context

**Not by sharing a store — by naming a room, and by classifying every field.**

`cross-user-sharing` is 12 modules built on rooms: `xuser-relay`, `xuser-remote-turns`,
`xuser-turn-dedupe-store`, `xuser-room-tombstone-store`, `xuser-departure-obligations`,
`xuser-pending-departure-store`, `xuser-state-reconcile`, `xuser-wire-normalization`. The
names carry the design: turns are deduped, rooms get tombstones, leaving has obligations
that outlive the leave, and the wire form is normalized separately from the local form.

The stronger mechanism is redaction. `packages/redaction` defines

    DataClassification: safe | code | credentials | path | provider_info | unspecified
    SENSITIVE_CLASSIFICATIONS = everything except safe, INCLUDING unspecified
    PrivacyMode: UNSPECIFIED=0 NO_STORAGE=1 NO_TRAINING=2
                 USAGE_DATA_TRAINING_ALLOWED=3 USAGE_CODEBASE_TRAINING_ALLOWED=4
    PrivacyCapability (purpose): storage_for_training | storage_for_logging
                               | storage_for_usage | unsafe_always_allowed

and one total function `allowedPurpose(privacyMode, purpose, classification) -> bool`:
`safe` always true; `unsafe_always_allowed` always true; **`credentials` and `unspecified`
always false**; `UNSPECIFIED` privacy mode denies exactly like `NO_STORAGE`; under
`NO_TRAINING` logging admits only `path` and `provider_info`; unknown purpose throws
against a TypeScript exhaustiveness variable.

`packages/redacted-protos` then generates **80 per-message codecs** (`toRedacted*` /
`fromRedacted*`) from a per-field classification table. Measured field counts across those
tables: 986 `SAFE`, 701 `CODE`, 318 `PATH`, 39 `CREDENTIALS`. Content-bearing fields become
`RedactedString` wrappers that are not strings until someone calls `.unwrap(purpose)`. A
new field with no classification is `unspecified`, and `unspecified` is denied.

## How agents talk to each other

**Three distinct mechanisms, deliberately not one.**

1. **Subagent (parent to child).** `agent.v1.SubagentPersistedState` carries
   `{conversation_state, created_timestamp_ms, last_used_timestamp_ms, subagent_type,
   model_id, environment, cloud_subagent, first_class_bc_id,
   cloud_requested_environment_build_id, machine}`. Delegation with persisted state and a
   target machine.

2. **Coordinator tools (peer to peer), exposed to the model as tools.** `CreateAgent`,
   `SendToAgent{tool_call_id, agent_id, message, delivery, title}`, `GetAgentStatus`,
   `ReadAgentTranscript`, `StopAgent` — each with `Args`/`Success`/`Error`/`Result`/`ToolCall`
   messages. In the classification table `agent_id` and `delivery` are `SAFE` while
   `message` and `title` are `CODE`: addressing is metadata, content is content.

3. **Group chat (many agents in one room).** About 120 lines, and the whole termination
   argument is in five constants: `GROUP_MAX_ROUNDS = 3`, `GROUP_MAX_MEMBER_TURNS = 10`,
   `GROUP_MAX_MESSAGES_PER_TURN = 2`, `GROUP_PROMPT_HISTORY_LIMIT = 24`,
   `SHARED_ROOM_HISTORY_LIMIT = 24`. `orderRoundSpeakers` rotates the speaking order by
   `round % members` so no member always speaks first. `resolveResponders` scans back to
   the last USER message and collects `@name` / `@everyone` / `@all` mentions since; if
   nobody was mentioned, everyone answers. `isPassContent` lets a member answer `pass` or
   `(pass)` and be treated as declining, which is what keeps a bounded round from
   manufacturing noise. Groups may not nest: `SandGroupNestingError` refuses with the
   remedy in the message.

---

## Five mechanisms to take into cloud-itonami-app

### 1. Approval has a lifetime, and its unit is the user's direction

`SandLocalToolPermissionController` is the piece worth copying whole. Its own comments
state the invariants:

- A new direction from the user **retires every approval the agent holds** and denies
  anything still waiting on an unanswered card, \"so consent can never leak across turns\".
  The agent's direction epoch moves forward; work still running keeps the epoch it
  captured.
- A refusal is remembered **with its epoch**, keyed by `sha256(target)` so the raw command
  or path is not retained. \"A refusal recorded before the caller's direction started is
  the user's older answer to older work, and the direction they have given since is free
  to ask again.\"
- Widening the setting to `always` **does not cover callers that began before the grant**;
  they ask as they would have.
- The exact-refusal memory is bounded (512 per agent). Eviction ages by DIRECTION first
  and only then by insertion order, and **every eviction raises a saturation marker so
  that direction and all older ones are refused wholesale**. \"The budget bounds MEMORY
  without ever bounding the guarantee.\"
- Deleting an agent invalidates before dropping bookkeeping, and leaves a tombstone so a
  call still unwinding is refused rather than read as unremarkable.

This is ADR-2608136000's rule — a check that could not measure must not return the pass
value — applied to consent. cloud-itonami-app's `bots/decide!` today records one decision
per card and has none of: expiry by direction, memory of refusals, or a safe answer when
that memory overflows. ADR-0044 fixed the adjacent defect (the app demanded an
authorization on turns that needed nothing); this is the same family and is not yet done.

### 2. Data classification is per field, generated, and unclassified means sensitive

`allowedPurpose` is three scalars in and one bool out, no effects, fail-closed on unknown
in BOTH arguments. That is the shape of a `kotoba/pure` decision core, and it is a better
first candidate than most of what is in `cloud-itonami-app/src/*.kotoba` today, because the
alternative is what the app has now: judgements about what may leave the machine spread
across `transcript`, `system-prompt` and each connector. ADR-2608130100 made \"named and
reviewed before it can happen\" the thesis; this is the mechanism that makes it checkable
per field instead of per code path.

### 3. Agent-to-agent needs three shapes, not one

cloud-itonami-app has `handoff` (one Bot to another) and nothing else. The three shapes
answer different questions — delegate and keep the result (subagent), address a peer and
carry on (coordinator), converse in a bounded room (group). Merging them produces either a
handoff that cannot converse or a chat that cannot terminate. If a group form is added,
take the five constants and `pass` with it: those, not the prompt, are what make it stop.

### 4. Two primitives, about twenty lines each

`WriteEpoch` — `begin()` returns a settle function; `isStale(snapshotAtStart)` answers
\"did a write land between my read and now\". The stale-card and permanent
`waiting-connection` defects fixed in ADR-0044 were both this shape.

`assertPathOutsideProtectedRoots` — checks the resolved path AND its realpath against the
protected roots, resolving a not-yet-existing path to its nearest existing ancestor. A
symlink into a protected root is caught. Applies directly to Drive sync and attachment
staging.

### 5. Generate the identifiers, and check the list that cannot be generated

`extension-ids.generated.ts` derives 35 extension ids from the registry, so an extension
cannot be named by a string that does not exist. But `registry.ts` is a hand-written array
and nothing checks that the two agree. cloud-itonami-app's `test_runner.clj` already does
better — it refuses to run a suite that does not cover every test file on the path, and
its docstring records the measurement that made it necessary. **Keep that; do not copy the
weaker half.**

---

## What not to take

- **Do not take the box-provisioning shape as a model for a remote execution plane.**
  `EnsureSandBox` returns three bearer tokens and three URLs from one call, and the box
  then polls `/local-exec/requests` to ask the user's machine to run things. That is a
  reasonable product; it is the inverse of ADR-2608130100's thesis, where the policy has
  to reach the work.
- **Do not take `sand-secrets.json`'s file mode.** Its three values are Chromium OSCrypt
  envelopes (base64 of bytes beginning `v10`, keyed to the login Keychain), so 0644 is not
  an exposure — but the same app writes `gateway-descriptor.json` at 0600 and the
  local-exec daemon files at an explicit 0600. One place lost the discipline.
- **Do not read a shipped-but-uncalled binary as a capability.** `sand-op-launcher` can
  drive the 1Password CLI. Nothing in this artifact calls it. Whether the box side calls
  it, or it is dead weight from a shared build, cannot be determined from the bundle, and
  writing down either answer would be inventing evidence.

## Verification

- asar header parsed directly (16-byte header, JSON directory); 681 entries, 146 files
  extracted excluding `dist/deps`, to
  `scratchpad/grokbot/extracted/`.
- Service and message shapes read from the Connect-ES service definitions and
  `proto3.util.newFieldList` field tables in `host/host-main.cjs`.
- Field-classification counts by regex over the generated tables: SAFE 986, CODE 701,
  PATH 318, CREDENTIALS 39.
- `codesign -dv --verbose=4` and `codesign -d --entitlements`: Developer ID Anysphere
  (DCNK4UB866), hardened runtime, entitlements limited to apple-events, allow-jit, camera,
  audio-input; no App Sandbox; helpers run `--no-sandbox`.
- Secret-at-rest form determined structurally (base64 decode, first three bytes) without
  printing any value.

## Consequences

1. **Item 1 (approval lifetime) is DONE**, landed 2026-08-14 as
   `cloud-itonami-app` ADR-0046 (`abf546a`). The direction epoch is implemented:
   `send!` advances it, an approval card records the direction it was raised under, and
   `bot_core.kotoba/request-standing` decides where a held request stands from three
   scalars. It fixed a measured defect — a second message replaced the run while the card
   stayed undecided, so the Bot reported `waiting-approval` for the rest of the
   conversation and rendered a button whose only outcome was 承認待ちの操作がありません。

   **The hashed refusal key and the saturation marker were deliberately NOT built**, and
   this ADR's original wording asking for them was wrong to name them as requirements
   without checking reachability. Rejecting a held write clears the run, so nothing
   re-proposes inside one direction; in a later direction the refusal correctly would not
   hold; and routines and handoffs are refused by `may-start?` / `may-fire?` while a run
   is held. There is no path, and a check that cannot discriminate is theatre. ADR-0046
   records the condition under which it becomes reachable — a direction running
   concurrently with the person answering, or a rejection that stops clearing the run.
2. Item 2 (classification + `allowedPurpose`) is a `.kotoba` decision core candidate under
   the ADR-2608650000 rules: scalars in, bool out, `[:result T E]` instead of throw, and a
   parity test against the cljc caller.
3. Items 3-5 are recorded here and are not scheduled.
4. This ADR describes an external artifact at one version. If it is re-examined, re-measure
   rather than citing these numbers: v0.18.0 is a moving target and the counts above are
   dated 2026-08-14.")))
